  "statement": "Congress must reject invasive model-weight discovery and cryptographic tracking. Internal inspection of neural networks is a scientific impossibility. Mechanistic interpretability research shows that mapping billions of parameters to causal pathways is computationally intractable. Demanding model weights in court is like demanding a driver's brain synapses after a car crash. Courts must rely on API-level behavioral audits and black-box testing instead. Federal courts can already compel this output data under Rule 34 of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure. This approach protects intellectual property while delivering clear evidence of harm. This policy benefits startups and consumers while placing the burden of proof on actual performance. The Clean Air Act of 1970 set tailpipe emissions standards rather than forcing car companies to surrender proprietary engine formulas. Output-based verification must remain the standard for algorithmic accountability.\n\nMandatory cryptographic audit trails will destroy open-source AI and entrench a permanent oligopoly of tech giants. Regulatory compliance costs act as a regressive tax that disproportionately burdens small developers. European Union GDPR data shows that small businesses faced a 30% increase in administrative costs after its 2018 implementation. Meanwhile, Google increased its market share in online advertising. A federal mandate for cryptographic, subject-keyed audit trails would require a full-time compliance team for every single deployment. This requirement would immediately bankrupt independent developers and open-source projects. The Federal Trade Commission would enforce these rules, driving up administrative overhead. A coalition of venture capitalists and open-source advocates opposes this burden, while corporate lobbyists support it. The Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 cost small public companies an average of $2.3 million annually and caused a 50% drop in new US public listings. Congress must reject cryptographic tracking mandates to prevent the total centralization of the AI ecosystem.\n\nWarping tort law to penalize algorithmic systems for inevitable errors while ignoring their massive statistical benefits is a moral failure. Safety advocates correctly argue that black-box models make it difficult for injured plaintiffs to prove causation under traditional design-defect standards. That is a real challenge. But the solution is not to expand discovery into proprietary code. If a clinical AI tool like Google's LYNA reduces breast cancer misdiagnosis rates by 30% but makes a single incorrect prediction, strict liability would bankrupt the developer. This legal structure forces developers to pull life-saving tools from the market. Thousands of patients continue to die from human errors that the AI could have prevented. This argument assumes that private insurance markets can price algorithmic risk effectively without government intervention. State and federal courts must apply flexible foreseeability standards under the Restatement of Torts. The National Childhood Vaccine Injury Act of 1986 protected vaccine manufacturers from design-defect liability to prevent critical drug shortages. Existing common law tort doctrines must adapt to preserve these life-saving gains.",
