  "statement": "Proponents of rapid deployment argue that strict liability will freeze innovation. They claim that holding developers liable for every edge-case failure ignores the net positive lives saved by autonomous medical or transport systems. However, existing tort law fails to govern artificial intelligence because algorithmic fault fragments across developers, deployers, and users. This fragmentation leaves victims without recourse and creates systemic instability. The \"Problem of Many Hands\" dictates that when collective systems cause harm, diffuse responsibility prevents accountability. Traditional negligence standards, outlined in \"How Existing Liability Frameworks Can Handle Agentic AI Harms\", require proving a developer failed to act as a reasonable person. This standard is impossible to meet when downstream fine-tuners alter the model. When a medical system misdiagnoses a patient, the manufacturer blames the hospital's data, the hospital blames the software developer, and the developer blames the user. This fragmentation leaves injured patients bearing the entire cost, while tech companies pocket the profits. Congress must establish a strict joint-and-several liability framework. The Federal Trade Commission will enforce this by prosecuting upstream developers who fail to certify their systems. A coalition of consumer advocacy groups and insurance consortia supports this reform, while tech trade groups oppose it. The historical precedent is the Oil Pollution Act of 1990, which forced tanker operators to internalize environmental costs.\n\nOpponents of cryptographic tracking argue that mandatory audit trails create a centralized surveillance state. They fear compliance costs will destroy open-source development and concentrate power in a few tech giants. Yet, cryptographic, subject-keyed audit trails do not destroy open-source innovation. They protect it by securing supply chains against upstream poisoning and untraceable exploits. AI supply chains